SSUUBBSSCCRRIIBBEERR OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     openresolv ships with subscribers for the name servers dnsmasq(8),
     named(8), pdnsd(8), pdns_recursor(8), and unbound(8).  Each subscriber
     can create configuration files which should be included in in the sub‐
     scribers main configuration file.

     To disable a subscriber, simply set it's name to NO.  For example, to
     disable the libc subscriber you would set:

     libc=NO

     ddnnssmmaassqq__ccoonnff
             This file tells dnsmasq which name servers to use for specific
             domains.

     ddnnssmmaassqq__rreessoollvv
             This file tells dnsmasq which name servers to use for global
             lookups.

             Example resolvconf.conf for dnsmasq:

             name_servers=127.0.0.1 dnsmasq_conf=/etc/dnsmasq-conf.conf dns‐
             masq_resolv=/etc/dnsmasq-resolv.conf

             Example dnsmasq.conf:

             listen-address=127.0.0.1 # If dnsmasq is compiled for DBus then
             we can take # advantage of not having to restart dnsmasq.  en‐
             able-dbus conf-file=/etc/dnsmasq-conf.conf resolv-file=/etc/dns‐
             masq-resolv.conf

     nnaammeedd__ooppttiioonnss
             Include this file in the named options block.  This file tells
             named which name servers to use for global lookups.

     nnaammeedd__zzoonneess
             Include this file in the named global scope, after the options
             block.  This file tells named which name servers to use for spe‐
             cific domains.

             Example resolvconf.conf for named:

             name_servers=127.0.0.1 named_options=/etc/named-options.conf
             named_zones=/etc/named-zones.conf

             Example named.conf:

             options {      listen-on { 127.0.0.1; };      include
             "/etc/named-options.conf"; };

             include "/etc/named-zones.conf";

     ppddnnssdd__ccoonnff
             This is the main pdnsd configuration file which we modify to add
             our forward domains to.  If this variable is not set then we rely
             on the pdnsd configuration file setup to read _p_d_n_s_d___r_e_s_o_l_v as
             documented below.

     ppddnnssdd__rreessoollvv
             This file tells pdnsd about global name servers.  If this vari‐
             able is not set then it's written to _p_d_n_s_d___c_o_n_f.

             Example resolvconf.conf for pdnsd:

             name_servers=127.0.0.1 pdnsd_conf=/etc/pdnsd.conf # pdnsd_re‐
             solv=/etc/pdnsd-resolv.conf

             Example pdnsd.conf:

             global {      server_ip = 127.0.0.1;      status_ctl = on; }
             server {      # A server definition is required, even if empty.
                  label="empty";      proxy_only=on;      # file="/etc/pdnsd-
             resolv.conf"; }

     ppddnnss__zzoonneess
             This file tells pdns_recursor about specific and global name
             servers.

             Example resolvconf.conf for pdns_recursor:

             name_servers=127.0.0.1 pdns_zones=/etc/pdns/recursor-zones.conf

             Example recursor.conf:

             allow-from=127.0.0.0/8, ::1/128 forward-zones-file=/etc/pdns/re‐
             cursor-zones.conf

     uunnbboouunndd__ccoonnff
             This file tells unbound about specific and global name servers.

     uunnbboouunndd__iinnsseeccuurree
             When set to YES, unbound marks the domains as insecure, thus ig‐
             noring DNSSEC.

             Example resolvconf.conf for unbound:

             name_servers=127.0.0.1 unbound_conf=/etc/unbound-resolvconf.conf

             Example unbound.conf:

             include: /etc/unbound-resolvconf.conf

SSUUBBSSCCRRIIBBEERR IINNTTEEGGRRAATTIIOONN
     Not all distributions store the files the subscribers need in the same
     locations.  For example, named service scripts have been called named,
     bind and rc.bind and they could be located in a directory called
     /etc/rc.d, /etc/init.d or similar.  Each subscriber attempts to automati‐
     cally configure itself, but not every distribution has been catered for.
     Also, users could equally want to use a different version from the one
     installed by default, such as bind8 and bind9.  To accommodate this, the
     subscribers have these files in configurable variables, documented below.

     ddnnssmmaassqq__sseerrvviiccee
             Name of the dnsmasq service.

     ddnnssmmaassqq__rreessttaarrtt
             Command to restart the dnsmasq service.

     ddnnssmmaassqq__ppiidd
             Location of the dnsmasq pidfile.

     lliibbcc__sseerrvviiccee
             Name of the libc service.

     lliibbcc__rreessttaarrtt
             Command to restart the libc service.

     nnaammeedd__sseerrvviiccee
             Name of the named service.

     nnaammeedd__rreessttaarrtt
             Command to restart the named service.

     ppddnnssdd__rreessttaarrtt
             Command to restart the pdnsd service.

     ppddnnss__sseerrvviiccee
             Command to restart the pdns_recursor service.

     ppddnnss__rreessttaarrtt
             Command to restart the pdns_recursor service.

     uunnbboouunndd__sseerrvviiccee
             Name of the unbound service.

     uunnbboouunndd__rreessttaarrtt
             Command to restart the unbound service.

     uunnbboouunndd__ppiidd
             Location of the unbound pidfile.

